[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: ispa, ispb
diagnosis_text:
Internet loss is caused by an ISP-side routing misconfiguration, not by R1 or the uplinks. Hosts in Office A (e.g., PC1) have local L2/L3 working: PC1 reaches its default gateway 10.1.0.1 on dswa1 and can reach R1’s WAN IP 203.0.113.2 (pc1→203.0.113.2 ping succeeds). NAT on R1 is configured (MASQUERADE rules present), and R1’s default points to ISPa (default via 203.0.113.1). However, beyond ISPa traffic dies: from R1, traceroute to 1.1.1.1 hits 203.0.113.1 and then times out; ISPa itself cannot ping 1.1.1.1.

Direct evidence localizes the fault to ISPa’s routing: ISPa’s routing table shows “default via 203.0.113.2 dev eth0” — back to R1. ISPb is similarly misconfigured (“default via 203.0.113.6 dev eth0”). This creates a routing loop for Internet destinations: R1 forwards to ISPa, which forwards back to R1 (and/or to ISPb which also forwards back), so packets never leave the customer/ISP edge and time out. That’s why internal pings and to R1’s WAN work, but any off-net address (e.g., 1.1.1.1, example.com) fails.

Root cause: ISPa (and also ISPb) have their default route erroneously pointing to the customer router R1, creating a loop and blackholing Internet-bound traffic.

Corrective fix: Do not restart R1. Correct ISPa’s default route to its true upstream (e.g., replace with “ip route replace default via 192.0.2.2 dev eth1” toward ISPb or appropriate upstream) and remove the default-to-R1 on ISPb as well. After updating, restart networking on ISPa/ISPb if needed to apply cleanly.
=== END ===